#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# rasterize

#% text
# `rasterize(x)` makes `x` a picture: graphics are rendered, other values drawn as their text.

#% text
# `rasterize(x, p)` gives property `p` of the picture: `"RasterSize"`, `"ImageSize"`, `"BoundingBox"`, `"Data"`, `"Regions"`, `"Image"`, or a list of them.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Sizes are in pixels, one pixel a point. `"BoundingBox"` is `[w, h, b]` with `b` the height above the baseline; a picture's baseline is its bottom edge, so `b` is `h`.

#% item
# `"Data"` is the pixels as rows of `[r, g, b]` triples (0 to 255), the top row first.

#% item
# `"Regions"` gives `[tag, "Region"] -> [[left, top], [right, bottom]]` for each `annotation(prim, tag, "Region")` of a graphic, in pixels with y up from the bottom edge.

#% item
# `"Properties"` lists the property names; an unknown name gives `failure(unknown_property, ...)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
rasterize(graphics([red, disk()]))
#% output
#= image("rasterize.files/rasterize-1.svgz")

#% cell
rasterize(x^2 + 1)
#% output
#= image("rasterize.files/rasterize-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The size of a picture, and the regions of its annotated parts:

#% cell
g = graphics([red, annotation(disk(), "Red", "Region"), blue, annotation(disk([3, 0]), "Blue", "Region")], image_size: 100);

#% cell
rasterize(g, ["RasterSize", "BoundingBox"])
#% output
#= [[100, 45], [100, 45, 45]]

#% cell
rasterize(g, "Regions")
#% output
#= [["Red", "Region"] -> [[6, 40], [41, 5]], ["Blue", "Region"] -> [[59, 40], [94, 5]]]

#% text
# The colour of a pixel in the middle of the red disk:

#% cell
rasterize(g, "Data")[23, 23]
#% output
#= [255, 0, 0]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `plot`
